course: dictionary
lesson: 14318
title: Obliged: must, thank you, or helped
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Obliged is an adjective, and it is also the past form of the verb oblige. It has a few meanings, so use the context to find the right one. Ask yourself: is someone being made to do something, is someone thanking someone, or is someone helping?
explain: After «to», obliged means «must» or «having to». «You are obliged to wear a seat belt in the car.» The clue is the rule: a rule makes it something you must do.
explain: In polite speech, «much obliged» means «thank you». «Thanks for the lift, I'm much obliged.» The clue is the thanks. As a verb, «oblige» means to help someone by doing what they ask: «The shop assistant obliged us by finding our size.»
fact: Obliged means having to do something because of a rule, law or duty.
fact: «Obliged to» means «must», as in «obliged to stop at a red light».
fact: «I'm much obliged» is an old-fashioned way of saying «Thank you very much».
fact: To oblige someone means to help them by doing what they ask.
fact: The verb oblige changes to obliged in the past: «She obliged us yesterday.»
exercise: «You are obliged to wear a seat belt in the car.» What does obliged mean here? | You would like to, if you feel like it | You are sad about the belt | *You have to, because it is a rule | You are thankful for the belt
exercise: «I'm much obliged to you for helping me.» What does obliged mean here? | Annoyed | *Thankful | Forced to do something | Tired
exercise: Find the word in «The shop assistant obliged us by finding our size.» that means helped by doing what we asked. | shop | size | finding | *obliged
test: «Drivers are obliged to stop at a red light.» What is the best meaning? | They stop because they are thankful | They stop because they are tired | *They must stop, because it is the rule | They may stop if they like
test: Sam says «I'm much obliged» after Ali holds the door open. What does Sam mean? | I am going out | *Thank you very much | I must hold the door now | I am sad about the door
exam: «Visitors are ___ to sign in at the desk.» Which word fits best? | *obliged | curt | barely | famished
